(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-johnlewis"]={"plain":{"bills":{"johnlewis":{"slug":"johnlewis","title":"The John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-101","heading":"\u00a7 101. The results test, restated","part":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"**(a) Violation.** A violation is established where, based on the totality of circumstances, the political processes leading to nomination or election are not equally open to participation by members of a class protected by this section, in that its members have less opportunity than other members of the electorate to participate in the political process and to elect representatives of their choice.\n\n**(b) Intent is not an element.** **Proof of discriminatory purpose is not required**, and no inference to the contrary may be drawn from the absence of such proof. Where purpose is proved it is sufficient, and this subsection does not limit any claim so proved.\n\n**(c) The circumstances that may be considered are not closed.** No factor is a prerequisite, no factor is dispositive, and **a court may not require a plaintiff to negate any explanation the defendant has not offered.**\n\n**(d) Practices in combination.** Where two or more practices operate together, the effect of their operation together is the effect to be assessed, **and a defendant may not require that each be assessed alone.**\n\n**(e) Availability of an alternative.** That a member of a protected class was able to vote by some other means does not defeat a claim that a practice imposed a disparate burden.\n\n**(f) A partisan explanation does not defeat a claim under this section.** That a practice was adopted to advantage or disadvantage a political party is **one circumstance among the totality** and is **not, standing alone, a sufficient answer** to a showing that the practice results in members of a protected class having less opportunity to participate and to elect representatives of their choice. **Where the racial and partisan composition of an electorate are correlated, the correlation is a fact to be weighed and not a defense to be accepted.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"*Amends section 2 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 10301.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-102","heading":"\u00a7 102. Retrogression","part":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"**(a)** A voting change that would leave members of a protected class worse off than the practice it replaces, with respect to their opportunity to participate and to elect representatives of their choice, violates section 2.\n\n**(b)** The comparison is to the practice actually in force, and **not to a practice the jurisdiction might lawfully have adopted instead.**"},{"anchor":"sec-103","heading":"\u00a7 103. Relief","part":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"**(a) Interim relief.** In an action under section 2 relating to an election, where the court acts on or after the 90th day before the first day on which any ballot may be cast in that election, the court shall consider the proximity of the election as one factor among others, **and proximity alone is not a ground for denying relief.**\n\n**(b) No blanket rule of repose.** No court shall adopt a categorical rule barring relief within a fixed period before an election."},{"anchor":"sec-104","heading":"\u00a7 104. Notice of voting changes","part":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"**(a) Duty.** A State or political subdivision that makes a change described in subsection (b) shall publish the change, in the manner stated in the SCHEDULE, not later than the period stated there before the election at which it first applies.\n\n**(b) Changes covered.** A change to the qualifications for voting; to the documentation required to register or to vote; to the method of election; to the boundaries of a district or precinct; to the location, hours or number of polling places; to the availability of voting by mail; or to the maintenance of registration lists.\n\n**(c) Consequence.** A change published later than this section requires **does not apply at that election**, and the practice in force before the change applies.\n\n**(d) Ministerial.** The duty to publish is ministerial and enforceable by any person.\n\n**(e) Changes not within subsection (c).** Subsection (c) does not apply to a change made to comply with an order of a court or with a requirement of Federal law. A change so made **shall be published as soon as practicable**, and the publication shall state the order or requirement it implements."},{"anchor":"sec-105","heading":"\u00a7 105. Observers","part":"TITLE I \u2014 RESTORATION OF SECTION 2","body":"**(a) The existing mechanism is extended, not duplicated.** The certification by which observers are assigned under section 8 of the Voting Rights Act may be made by the Attorney General **as to any election in a jurisdiction covered under Title II of this Act, and as to any election in which a court has ordered relief under this Act.** Observers so certified **are assigned in the same manner, and have the same authority, as observers assigned under that section.**\n\n**(b) Access.** An observer may enter and remain in a polling place and a place where ballots are counted, and may observe every stage of the conduct of the election. **This subsection states the authority conferred by section 8 and does not narrow it.**\n\n**(c) Not enforcement.** An observer exercises no law-enforcement authority, carries no weapon, and takes no action affecting any ballot, voter or official. **\u00a7 501 does not apply to an observer assigned under this section.**\n\n**(d) Cumulative.** The authority under this section is **in addition to** the authority to assign observers under any other provision of the Voting Rights Act, and **does not repeal, limit or replace it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-201","heading":"\u00a7 201. Coverage by recent violations","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a) State coverage.** A State and all of its political subdivisions are subject to section 5 for ten years where, in the preceding twenty-five years, there occurred **fifteen or more voting rights violations** in the State, or **ten or more** of which at least one was committed by the State itself.\n\n**(b) Subdivision coverage.** A political subdivision is separately subject to section 5 for ten years where **three or more voting rights violations** occurred there in the preceding twenty-five years.\n\n**(c) Rolling, and by operation of law.** The period is measured continuously. A violation counts on the date it becomes final: for a judgment, when it is no longer subject to appeal; for a consent decree, when it is entered; for a settlement, when it is executed; and for an objection, when it is interposed. **Coverage begins on the earlier of the date the Attorney General publishes the determination under \u00a7 203(a) and the 30th day after the date on which the violation completing the count in subsection (a) or (b) becomes final.** Any person may establish, in an action under \u00a7 905(a) on the public record, that coverage has begun and the date on which it began.\n\n**(d) Fallback.** If the beginning of coverage otherwise than on publication is held invalid, coverage begins on publication of the determination under \u00a7 203(a), which remains a ministerial duty due on the date that subsection fixes.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"*Amends sections 3(c), 4, and 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, 52 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 10302(c), 10303, 10304.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-201a","heading":"\u00a7 201A. The bail-in that survived","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(b) The trigger is amended.** Section 3(c) is amended so that a court may enter an order under that section on a finding of a violation of **section 2 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, as restated by Title I**, and not only on a finding of a violation of the Fourteenth or Fifteenth Amendment.\n\n**(d) Cumulative.** Coverage under \u00a7 201 or \u00a7 202 does not limit relief under section 3(c), and relief under section 3(c) does not limit coverage under this Title."},{"anchor":"sec-202","heading":"\u00a7 202. Practice-based coverage","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a)** Regardless of \u00a7 201, a covered practice is subject to preclearance in any jurisdiction meeting the demographic threshold stated in the SCHEDULE.\n\n**(b) Covered practices.** Changes to the method of election in a jurisdiction with racially polarized voting \u00b7 redistricting \u00b7 annexation altering the composition of the electorate \u00b7 reduction, consolidation or relocation of polling places \u00b7 changes to registration list maintenance \u00b7 changes to documentation required to register or to vote \u00b7 and reductions in multilingual voting materials.\n\n**(c) These and only these.** The omission of a practice from subsection (b) is a determination by Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-202a","heading":"\u00a7 202A. Section 5 reaches this coverage","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"Section 5(a) of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 (52 U.S.C. 10304(a)) is amended by inserting, after \"different from that in force or effect on November 1, 1972,\", the following, so that the first sentence of that subsection reads, through the words amended, as follows, with new language marked as inserted and the remainder of the subsection unchanged:\n\n> Whenever a State or political subdivision with respect to which the prohibitions set forth in section 4(a) based upon determinations made under the first sentence of section 4(b) are in effect shall enact or seek to administer any voting qualification or prerequisite to voting, or standard, practice, or procedure with respect to voting different from that in force or effect on November 1, 1964, or whenever a State or political subdivision with respect to which the prohibitions set forth in section 4(a) based upon determinations made under the second sentence of section 4(b) are in effect shall enact or seek to administer any voting qualification or prerequisite to voting, or standard, practice, or procedure with respect to voting different from that in force or effect on November 1, 1968, or whenever a State or political subdivision with respect to which the prohibitions set forth in section 4(a) based upon determinations made under the third sentence of section 4(b) are in effect shall enact or seek to administer any voting qualification or prerequisite to voting, or standard, practice, or procedure with respect to voting different from that in force or effect on November 1, 1972, **or whenever a State or political subdivision with respect to which coverage is in effect under section 201 of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act shall enact or seek to administer any voting qualification or prerequisite to voting, or standard, practice, or procedure with respect to voting, different from that in force or effect on the date that coverage began, or whenever a State or political subdivision with respect to which coverage is in effect under section 202 of that Act shall enact or seek to administer a change described in section 202(b) of that Act, different from the practice in force or effect on the date that coverage was first published under section 203(a-1) of that Act, in each case as to an election to which that Act applies under its section 904,** such State or subdivision may institute an action in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia for a declaratory judgment \u2026"},{"anchor":"sec-203","heading":"\u00a7 203. Publication","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a) Determinations under \u00a7 201.** The Attorney General shall publish in the Federal Register a determination that a jurisdiction is covered under \u00a7 201 not later than 30 days after the violation completing the count stated in that section becomes final, stating the violations counted and the date coverage begins and ends. Any person may notify the Attorney General of a violation, and as to a violation in an action to which the United States was not a party the 30 days run from the later of finality and the receipt of that notice; this sentence governs only the time for publication and does not delay coverage under \u00a7 201(c).\n\n**(a-1) Determinations under \u00a7 202.** The Director of the Census shall determine, and publish in the Federal Register not later than 90 days after the release of each decennial census and of each annual estimate from which the threshold can be computed, the jurisdictions meeting the demographic threshold stated in the SCHEDULE, and the Attorney General shall include those jurisdictions, and the practices covered as to each, in the publication required by subsection (b).\n\n**(a-2) Review of a determination.** A determination under subsection (a) or (a-1) that a jurisdiction is covered is not reviewable in any court, as the final paragraph of section 4(b) of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 provides for a determination under that section, and a jurisdiction contesting the count of violations attributed to it may proceed under \u00a7 205. If the preceding sentence is held invalid, a jurisdiction may contest the count of violations attributed to it only in an action in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, and coverage is not stayed during the action.\n\n**(b) Annual publication.** Not later than January 31 of each year, and as of the preceding December 31, the Attorney General shall publish the jurisdictions covered under \u00a7 201 and \u00a7 202, the violations counted, and the date coverage of each begins and ends; the number of objections withdrawn by the Attorney General or overturned by a final judgment of a court during the preceding calendar year, and their effect on the counts under \u00a7 201; and, for the preceding calendar year, the statistics required by subsections (c) and (d).\n\n**(c) Review statistics.** **The publication shall state the number of submissions, and of voting changes, received under section 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965; the number of changes to which an objection was interposed; the number of changes as to which the sixty-day period elapsed without a determination; the number of submissions as to which a request for further information was made, and the number as to which a new period was commenced under any regulation; and the time from receipt to determination at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.** For purposes of this section, a submission, and each change it contains, is received on the date the submission is first received, and a request for further information, a response to one, or a resubmission does not change that date; a determination is a written objection or a written statement that no objection is interposed; and a change as to which the period elapsed without a determination is counted in each percentile at the number of days from first receipt to the end of the period, and not fewer than sixty. This subsection governs the statistics this section requires and does not change when a period under section 5 begins or ends.\n\n**(d) Shortfall.** **Where the voting changes as to which the sixty-day period elapsed without a determination exceed 5 percent of all voting changes whose sixty-day period ended during the preceding calendar year, the publication shall state that the share was exceeded and by how much**, and shall state the number of full-time-equivalent reviewers that, at the average number of voting changes determined per full-time equivalent over the preceding three calendar years, or over the years since the first submission under \u00a7 201 or \u00a7 202 if fewer, would determine every change received in the preceding calendar year within sixty days of first receipt; where no change was determined in that period, the publication shall so state and shall state the number of full-time equivalents who performed review during the year. In an action under \u00a7 905(a), or under section 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, concerning a voting change as to which the sixty-day period elapsed during a year in which the share was exceeded, that excess is evidence that review staffing was inadequate during that year. The statement and the number are restated in each annual publication until a year in which the share is not exceeded. Notwithstanding section 1108(e) of title 31, United States Code, the Attorney General shall transmit them, including the personnel needs they state, on the date of the publication, to the Committees on the Judiciary of the Senate and of the House of Representatives. No officer or employee of an agency may condition or impair the transmittal, and nothing in this subsection prevents the Attorney General or the President from transmitting their own views. If the bar in the preceding sentence on conditioning or impairing the transmittal is held invalid, the duty to transmit stands.\n\n**(e) Register.** The Attorney General shall maintain and publish, within 10 days of each event, a register stating for each submission the jurisdiction, the changes submitted, the date of first receipt, the date of any request for further information and of any response, and the date and nature of the determination as to each change or that the sixty-day period elapsed without one. The statistics in this section are computed from the register.\n\n**(f) Continuity.** Review of submissions under section 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 is an activity authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for it are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of sections 1341 and 1342 of title 31, United States Code. The employees who perform it are excepted from furlough, and the Attorney General shall identify them in the Department of Justice's published plan for a lapse. The publication shall state separately the changes whose period elapsed during a lapse.\n\n**(g) Enforcement.** The duties in this section are ministerial and are requirements of Title II, enforceable in an action under \u00a7 905(a) brought in a district court of the United States as \u00a7 905(f)(6) provides. The court shall order performance of each element this section requires, and a person denied a publication or the register is aggrieved within the meaning of \u00a7 905(a-1)."},{"anchor":"sec-204","heading":"\u00a7 204. What counts as a violation","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a)** A violation includes a judgment, a consent decree, a settlement conditioned on a change in practice, and an objection interposed by the Attorney General.\n\n**(b) A withdrawal counts.** A voting change withdrawn, rescinded or abandoned after an action was filed **counts as a violation** for purposes of \u00a7 201, and the jurisdiction may not defeat the count by abandoning the practice.\n\n**(c)** A finding under Title III counts as a violation."},{"anchor":"sec-205","heading":"\u00a7 205. Bailout","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a) The existing procedure governs.** A jurisdiction covered under this Title may be relieved of coverage by a declaratory judgment of the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, **on the conditions and by the procedure provided in section 4(a) of the Voting Rights Act**, applied to coverage under this Title.\n\n**(a-1) One addition.** In addition to those conditions, the jurisdiction shall show that **it complied with every requirement of this Act** during the same period.\n\n**(b) The burden is the jurisdiction's**, and a bailout does not prevent later coverage on later violations.\n\n**(c) Cumulative.** This section is **in addition to** any procedure for termination of coverage under any other provision of the Voting Rights Act, and **does not repeal, limit or replace it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-206","heading":"\u00a7 206. Authority of the Attorney General","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRACTICE-BASED PRECLEARANCE","body":"**(a)** The Attorney General may bring an action to enforce any provision of this Act, and may intervene as of right in any action brought under it.\n\n**(b) Not a precondition.** No action by the Attorney General is a prerequisite to any private action under this Act, and a decision by the Attorney General not to act is not a bar, an adjudication, or evidence in any private action.\n\n**(c) Cumulative.** This section is **in addition to** the Attorney General's authority under any other provision of the Voting Rights Act, and **does not repeal, limit or replace it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-301","heading":"\u00a7 301. Standards for congressional districts","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"**(a) Duty.** A congressional district shall be drawn on the following criteria and no others \u2014 population equality; contiguity; compactness; respect for the boundaries of political subdivisions and of communities of interest; and compliance with the Voting Rights Act.\n\n**(a-1) Compactness is measured by a published metric.** The Director of the Census shall publish one or more metrics of district compactness and the method of computing each. **A State may use any published metric and shall state which it used.** Where a State states none, compactness is measured by the metric the Director designates as the default. **The metrics, their methods, and the default in effect on January 1 of the year following a decennial census govern every plan drawn on that census**, and a metric, method or default published later applies from the next census.\n\n**(b) Prohibited purpose and effect.** A districting plan may not be drawn **with the purpose, or with the effect, of denying or diluting the equal opportunity of any group of voters to participate in the political process and to elect representatives of their choice.**\n\n**(c) The group need not be defined by race.** Subsection (b) applies to a group of voters defined by political affiliation, by race, by color, by membership in a language minority, or by residence in a defined geographic area, and **no plaintiff is required to plead or prove race.** **The groups stated in this subsection are the only ones on which a claim under subsection (b) may rest, and the omission of a characteristic is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(d) The standard, stated so that it can be applied.** The effect described in subsection (b) is established where, across the range of election results actually observed in the State in the preceding ten years, **a plan produces a persistent and substantial divergence between the share of seats a group's preferred candidates would win and the share of votes those candidates would receive**, and the divergence is not explained by the criteria in subsection (a-1) and subsection (a).\n\n**(d-1) Comparison to alternatives.** A divergence is not explained by subsection (a) where a plan satisfying those criteria at least as well, and producing a materially smaller divergence, was available. **The comparison is to plans the State could have drawn, and the plaintiff may establish availability by producing one.** An alternative satisfies the criterion of compactness at least as well where it is at least as compact on the metric the State stated under subsection (a-1), or on the default metric, at the plaintiff's election.\n\n**(d-2) One election does not establish it.** A divergence observed in a single election is not sufficient.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"*Enacted under Article I, section 4. **This Title uses no racial classification and requires none.***"}]},{"anchor":"sec-301a","heading":"\u00a7 301A. Race and party may not be traded for one another","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"**(b) A partisan explanation is not a defense under this Title.** That a plan was drawn to advantage or disadvantage a political party **is not a defense to a claim under \u00a7 301(b)**, because that subsection forbids the dilution of the equal opportunity of **any** group of voters. **A plan that dilutes on partisan grounds and a plan that dilutes on racial grounds are each prohibited, and a defendant may not answer one by admitting the other.**\n\n**(c) Correlation does not convert one into the other.** Where the racial composition and the partisan composition of an electorate are correlated \u2014\n\n**(1)** evidence that the drafters used partisan data **does not negate** a finding under \u00a7 301(b); and\n\n**(2)** evidence that the drafters used racial data **does not require** one.\n\n**The question under \u00a7 301(b) is the purpose or effect of the plan upon a group of voters, and the label attached to the data used to achieve it does not answer that question.**\n\n**(d) Proxies.** Use of any datum that predicts the racial composition of an electorate \u2014 including partisan registration, primary participation, turnout history, language, surname, or geography \u2014 **with the purpose or effect described in \u00a7 301(b)** is within that subsection **regardless of the characteristic the datum is said to measure.**\n\n**(d-1) This subsection adds no prohibition of its own.** It does not make the use of any datum unlawful. **It provides only that the label attached to a datum does not remove conduct from \u00a7 301(b), and conduct not within \u00a7 301(b) is not reached by this section.** The ordinary use of political data in drawing a plan that does not produce the effect described in \u00a7 301(b) is unaffected.\n\n**(e) No requirement to disprove an alternative.** A plaintiff is not required to negate a partisan explanation, and a court may not require a plaintiff to establish that race rather than party was the predominant consideration.\n\n**(f) Rule of construction.** Nothing in this section requires or authorizes the use of any racial classification in drawing a district, and no plan is required by this section to contain any district drawn on the basis of race."},{"anchor":"sec-301b","heading":"\u00a7 301B. The record of how the map was drawn","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"**(a) Publication.** The authority adopting a congressional districting plan shall publish, with the plan and before its first use: the criteria applied and the metric stated under \u00a7 301(a-1); the data used, by source and type; each alternative plan considered by the adopting authority, by a member or employee of it, or by any person acting on its behalf or at the request of a member, with its computed divergence where one was computed; and the roll-call vote of each member of the adopting body.\n\n**(b) The record is what makes \u00a7 301 litigable.** In an action under \u00a7 302, the State may not rely on a criterion, datum, or consideration **absent from the published record** to explain a divergence under \u00a7 301(d), and an alternative produced by a plaintiff under \u00a7 301(d-1) is compared against the record as published.\n\n**(c) Ministerial.** The duty to publish is ministerial and enforceable by any person, and a person denied the record is aggrieved within the meaning of \u00a7 905(a-1)."},{"anchor":"sec-302","heading":"\u00a7 302. Action","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"**(a)** A voter in the State may bring an action in the district court, which shall be heard by a three-judge court.\n\n**(b) Remedy.** The court may enjoin use of the plan and, where the legislature does not enact a conforming plan within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, shall adopt one."},{"anchor":"sec-303","heading":"\u00a7 303. Severability from Title I","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RACE-NEUTRAL REDISTRICTING STANDARDS","body":"**This Title is independent of Title I and of Title II.** **Congress would have enacted this Title had it enacted no other Title of this Act**, and its operation does not depend on the validity of any provision restoring or construing section 2."},{"anchor":"sec-401","heading":"\u00a7 401. The right","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":"**(a)** A person entitled to vote in an election for federal office may cast that vote by mail, **without excuse and without stating a reason.**\n\n**(b) No condition.** A State may not condition voting by mail on age, on absence from the jurisdiction, on illness or disability, on notarization, or on a witness signature.\n\n**(c) Application.** A State may require an application, which shall be available without charge and may be submitted by mail, in person, or electronically. **A State shall accept an application received not later than the time stated in the SCHEDULE.**\n\n**(d) A standing application.** A voter may state on an application that it applies to every later election for Federal office. An application so made **remains in effect until the voter revokes it, changes residence to an address outside the registrar's jurisdiction, or is removed from the official list of eligible voters in accordance with section 8 of the National Voter Registration Act of 1993 (52 U.S.C. 20507)**, and a State may not require a voter whose application remains in effect to apply again. The application form shall offer the statement.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-401a","heading":"\u00a7 401A. The model already exists for some voters","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":"**(a)** Federal law has for decades guaranteed absent uniformed services voters and overseas voters the right to register and vote absentee in federal elections, and required States to accept their ballots. **This Title extends to every voter the guarantee those voters already hold**, and is to be construed consistently with it.\n\n**(b) No reduction.** Nothing in this Title reduces any right of a voter under that law."},{"anchor":"sec-402","heading":"\u00a7 402. Deadlines","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":"**(a) The date of mailing controls, where the State so provides.** A State may provide that a ballot cast in an election for federal office is timely if it is shown to have entered the mail on or before the date of the election by its postmark, by any other indication of the postal service that it entered the mail, including a tracking scan, or by a dated declaration of the voter made with the ballot, whichever shows the earliest date, **and is received within the period the State specifies.** A State may so provide for any one or more of those indications.\n\n**(b) No federal provision is to be read to forbid it.** No provision of federal law establishing the date of a federal election shall be construed to require that a ballot be **received** by that date.\n\n**(c) Where a postmark is absent or illegible**, the ballot is presumed timely if received within the period stated in the SCHEDULE."},{"anchor":"sec-403","heading":"\u00a7 403. Cure","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":"**(a)** Where a ballot is rejected for a defect in a signature, a date, a required envelope, or any other information or material the voter is required to provide with the ballot, the election official shall notify the voter and afford an opportunity to cure, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE.\n\n**(b)** A ballot may not be rejected for a defect the voter was given no opportunity to cure."},{"anchor":"sec-404","heading":"\u00a7 404. Delivery","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VOTING BY MAIL","body":"**(a)** A State shall provide not fewer than the number of secure ballot drop locations stated in the SCHEDULE, and **postage on a returned ballot is prepaid.**\n\n**(b) Paid for.** There are appropriated to the Election Assistance Commission, **out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated and without fiscal year limitation**, such sums as are necessary to reimburse a State for the cost of prepaid postage and of drop locations required by this section, of the notice and opportunity to cure required by \u00a7 403, and of the polling places and drop locations required by \u00a7 801, and for interest under this subsection. **A requirement of this section does not take effect in a State until funds are available to reimburse it**; that sentence does not apply to \u00a7 403 or \u00a7 801. **Amounts appropriated by this subsection are available on the date of enactment and remain available until expended. The appropriation is not subject to apportionment delay, reserve, or programmatic withholding and is available notwithstanding sections 1512 and 1513 of title 31, United States Code; an obligation or expenditure of it to reimburse a cost this subsection describes, or for interest under this subsection, does not violate section 1517(a) of that title by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less; and a failure to request, apportion, allot, obligate, or disburse the amounts appropriated does not render them unavailable within the meaning of this subsection**, and no requirement of this section is delayed, suspended, or excused by such a failure. Section 1012 of the Impoundment Control Act of 1974 (2 U.S.C. 683) does not apply to the amounts appropriated by this subsection, and section 1013(b) of that Act (2 U.S.C. 684(b)) permits no deferral of them; for purposes of sections 1015 and 1016 of that Act (2 U.S.C. 686, 687), those amounts are budget authority required under that Act to be made available for obligation. Where a State has submitted a claim for reimbursement and the amount owed has not been paid in full within 90 days after its submission, **the amount unpaid bears interest at the rate determined under section 1961(a) of title 28, United States Code, for the calendar week preceding the 91st day, computed daily and compounded annually, from the 91st day until it is paid, whether or not a court so orders**, and the State may recover the amount owed and the interest in an action in the United States Court of Federal Claims, and the requirement remains in effect meanwhile. \u00a7 512 applies to the amounts appropriated by this subsection."},{"anchor":"sec-501","heading":"\u00a7 501. Presence prohibited","part":"TITLE V \u2014 FEDERAL LAW ENFORCEMENT AT POLLING PLACES","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** An officer or employee of the United States, and any person acting under federal authority, **may not be present at a polling place, an early voting site, a ballot drop location, a place where ballots are received, processed, counted or canvassed, or a place where results are certified**, during the period beginning on the 90th day before the first day on which any ballot may be cast in an election and ending when certification of the results of that election is complete.\n\n**(b) Armed or not.** Subsection (a) applies **whether or not the person is armed, uniformed, or identifiable as a federal officer.**\n\n**(c) The exceptions, and these only.** Subsection (a) does not apply to \u2014 a person present to cast that person's own ballot \u00b7 a person present at the **written request of the State's chief election official**, which shall state the purpose and duration and shall be published before the presence begins \u00b7 a person responding to a request for emergency assistance made by an election official \u00b7 or a person acting under an order of a court issued in an action to which the State is a party.\n\n**(d) The District of Columbia.** For the District, the Metropolitan Police Department stands in the position of a State or local police force and is not subject to subsection (a). **No other federal force is within this subsection.**\n\n**(e) No general law-enforcement exception.** Neither the existence of a criminal investigation, nor a suspicion that an offense has been or is being committed, is a purpose for which presence is permitted under subsection (c).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 FEDERAL LAW ENFORCEMENT AT POLLING PLACES","body":"*Amends chapter 29 of title 18, United States Code.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-502","heading":"\u00a7 502. Consequence, and it does not run through the Department of Justice","part":"TITLE V \u2014 FEDERAL LAW ENFORCEMENT AT POLLING PLACES","body":"**(a) Any person.** The prohibition in \u00a7 501 is enforceable in a civil action by **any person**, by a State, or by a State's chief election official, for injunctive relief and for fees.\n\n**(b) The action of an officer present in violation has no effect.** No ballot may be rejected, no voter removed from a roll, no count altered, and no certification withheld, on the basis of anything done by a person present in violation of \u00a7 501.\n\n**(c) Criminal liability unaffected.** Section 592 and section 593 of this title are unaffected, and **this section does not require or await any action by the Attorney General.**"},{"anchor":"sec-503","heading":"\u00a7 503. Federalization of the National Guard","part":"TITLE V \u2014 FEDERAL LAW ENFORCEMENT AT POLLING PLACES","body":"The National Guard of a State may not be called into federal service for any purpose relating to the conduct of an election, and a member of the Guard in federal service is subject to \u00a7 501."},{"anchor":"sec-511","heading":"\u00a7 511. No executive alteration of election procedure","part":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":"**(a) No effect.** No executive order, memorandum, or agency directive may condition the delivery, receipt, or counting of a ballot in an election for Federal office on federal validation of a State's voter registration list, **and no federal agency, including the United States Postal Service, may refuse or delay the carriage or delivery of an election mailing on that basis.**\n\n**(b) The window, and it binds the actor.** A federal directive altering any procedure of an election for Federal office \u2014 **however denominated, including guidance, a rule, a condition, or an instruction to a contractor** \u2014 issued, or taking effect, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE \u2014 **measured from the first day on which any ballot may be cast, not from election day** \u2014 **has no effect.** This subsection binds the directive and not a reviewing court: no injunction is required, the rule is not waivable by any party's consent or request for expedition, and it applies whoever holds office.\n\n**(c) Not reached.** This section does not reach an Act of Congress, an order of a court, or a State's own administration of its elections.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-512","heading":"\u00a7 512. The funding lever closed","part":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":"**(a)** Funds appropriated for payments to States for election administration, however denominated, **may not be withheld, conditioned, reduced, delayed, or terminated** except for a failure to meet a condition stated by Congress in the appropriation or in the Act authorizing the payments, on a written finding published before the action takes effect. **A directive of the President or of a federal agency is not such a condition, and no such action may be taken by reason of, in whole or in part, a State's compliance or noncompliance with any such directive, or its election administration, litigation position, or officials.** A finding stating a permitted ground does not defeat a showing that a prohibited reason was a reason for the action.\n\n**(b) Action.** A State denied funds in violation of subsection (a) may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States for declaratory and injunctive relief, including an order that the funds be paid, and for fees, **and the immunity of the United States from such an action is expressly waived.** Where the State seeks only the amounts withheld, it may bring the action in the United States Court of Federal Claims, and section 1491 of title 28, United States Code, does not channel an action for declaratory or injunctive relief under this subsection out of the district court. \u00a7 902 applies to any authority substituted for one held unavailable."},{"anchor":"sec-513","heading":"\u00a7 513. Ballots stay in the States' custody","part":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":"**(a) No seizure without prior judicial authorization.** No officer or employee of the United States may seize, remove, take custody of, examine, or direct the transfer of ballots, tabulation equipment, or election records in the custody of a State or political subdivision, **except pursuant to a warrant or order of a court of the United States, issued on a particularized showing, after notice to the State's chief election official.** An authorization under this subsection is **stayed automatically pending any review the State seeks**, and the materials remain in the State's custody during the stay.\n\n**(b) The military never.** A member of the armed forces, and a member of the National Guard in federal service, **may not take any role in the custody, transport, or examination of ballots or election records** \u2014 under the procedure of subsection (a) or under any other authority.\n\n**(c) Return.** Materials obtained under subsection (a) shall be returned within the period the authorization states, and copies retained only as it provides."},{"anchor":"sec-514","heading":"\u00a7 514. A fraud claim carries its data","part":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":"**(a) Stated basis.** A federal officer or agency asserting that ineligible persons registered or voted, whether or not the assertion states a number, where the assertion is offered as a basis for federal action, **shall publish the underlying data, the methodology, and the source within the period stated in the SCHEDULE.** An assertion whose data are not so published **may not be cited as the basis for any federal action**, including an application under \u00a7 513(a).\n\n**(b) Refusal is not concession.** A State's declination to furnish voter data \u2014 including a declination Title VI protects \u2014 **shall not be treated, by any officer or in any proceeding, as evidence of, or a concession to, the existence of fraud.**"},{"anchor":"sec-515","heading":"\u00a7 515. Federal money for equipment that checks a ballot before it is cast","part":"TITLE V-A \u2014 THE FEDERAL ROLE IN ELECTION ADMINISTRATION","body":"**(a) Payment.** The Election Assistance Commission shall pay to a State, or to a political subdivision of a State, the cost it bore of acquiring, including by lease, programming, testing, and maintaining **equipment that examines a ballot cast in person in an election for Federal office, at the place where it is cast and before it is cast, for votes for more candidates for an office than may be elected to it**, and that, where a ballot indicates such votes, notifies the voter there that it does and of the effect of casting it, and affords the voter the opportunity to correct it before it is cast, in the manner section 301(a)(1)(A)(iii) and (C) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(1)(A)(iii), (C)) describes for a ballot indicating votes for more than one candidate for a single office, **and that does not cast such a ballot unless the voter, after the notice, directs that it be cast, and no person other than the voter, or a person assisting the voter at the voter's request, may so direct.** A device by which a ballot is marked that does not permit such votes to be indicated is equipment within this subsection. Equipment acquired with a payment under this section is purchased with funds made available under subchapter II of chapter 209 of title 52, United States Code, for purposes of section 301(a)(3)(C) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(3)(C)). **A payment under this section is a ministerial act, is not an action requiring approval under section 20928 of title 52, United States Code, and is made by the Executive Director of the Commission. No action of the Commission under this section is an action requiring approval under that section.**\n\n**(b) New capacity, not replacement.** For purposes of this subsection, the ballots cast in person at a place are of two categories: ballots counted at the place, and ballots held there without being counted, including provisional ballots under section 302 of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21082). **The cost of equipment at a place is payable under subsection (a) only to the extent the equipment examines, as that subsection describes, a category of ballots cast in person at the place that no equipment at the place so examined on the date stated in the SCHEDULE**, and the cost of programming equipment is payable only where the programming causes it to examine a category of ballots that it did not so examine on that date. A place first used after that date, and serving any voter whom a place in use on that date served, is treated as each place in use on that date that served such a voter, and equipment at it is payable only to the extent none of those places had equipment that so examined the category. Where equipment at the place, or at any place it is treated as under the preceding sentence, so examined a category on that date, the cost payable for equipment examining another category is limited to the cost of the programming, or of the additional units, needed to examine that category. The cost of maintaining equipment, and of programming it for an election, is payable only for equipment whose acquisition was payable under this subsection, **and the cost of maintaining or leasing that equipment, or of programming it for an election, is payable only for the period stated in the SCHEDULE after it is placed as to its acquisition.** Equipment acquired to replace equipment whose acquisition was paid for under this section is not payable.\n\n**(c) No other payment for the same cost.** The amount paid for a cost is reduced by any other Federal payment the State or the political subdivision applied to the same cost, and the claimant shall so certify. **Where a contract prices equipment within subsection (a) together with other goods or services, the cost of that equipment is its price under the contract's unit prices, or, where the contract states none, under the vendor's published prices.** Section 902(a) and (b) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21142(a), (b)) applies to a payment under this section as it applies to a payment made under that Act.\n\n**(c-1) The claim.** A claim states the cost; the place; the equipment, by kind and number; the categories of ballots under subsection (b) examined at the place on the date stated in the SCHEDULE; for a place treated under subsection (b) as one or more places in use on that date, each such place and the categories examined at it on that date; the State or political subdivision that bore the cost; the certification subsection (c) requires; and any certification subsection (e-1) requires. **A claim is submitted when it is delivered to the Commission and states those matters; the Commission may prescribe a form, and a claim stating those matters is not incomplete for want of it.** A claim states a matter whether or not the statement of it is later found incorrect. Not later than 30 days after a claim is first delivered, the Commission shall notify the claimant of each matter the claim does not state; a claim not stating a matter so identified is submitted when a later delivery states it, and a matter not so identified within that period is treated as stated in determining when the claim is submitted. The Commission shall publish an electronic address for the purpose not later than 30 days after the date of enactment; a claim, or a statement under subsection (e-1), sent electronically to an address the Commission has published for the purpose is delivered when sent, unless it is sent more than 30 days after the Commission publishes a replacement for that address, and one sent by mail to the principal office of the Commission is delivered not later than the third day after mailing. A claim delivered more than once, or by more than one means, is one claim, submitted on the earliest delivery on which this subsection would treat it as submitted. The Commission shall pay a claim within 90 days of its submission; its review is by audit under section 902(b) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21142(b)) after payment, and an amount found not payable is recovered by offset against a later payment under this section to the same State or political subdivision, and, to the extent not so recovered, is owed to the United States.\n\n**(c-2) The price per unit.** The cost payable under subsection (a) for acquiring a unit of equipment may not exceed the cap for its kind; for a lease, the cap applies to the sum of the payments for the unit's use under every lease of it to the State or political subdivision, and any price it pays to buy the unit, excluding maintenance and programming a lease prices separately. The kinds are equipment that examines a ballot marked by other means, and a device by which a ballot is marked; a unit of both kinds is of the kind whose cap, computed without the prices of such units, is greater. **The cap for a kind is the median of the purchase prices per unit of that kind stated in the contracts to which a State is a party in force on the date stated in the SCHEDULE**, each contract counted once, at the median of the prices it stated on that date for a unit of that kind, increased by the percentage by which the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (all items, United States city average, not seasonally adjusted) published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics for the month before the State or political subdivision became obligated to buy or lease the unit exceeds that index for the month in which that date falls. A contract is counted where, not later than 60 days after the date of enactment, a State has published it, any person has furnished it to the Commission, or the Commission has obtained it. Within 15 days after the date of enactment, the Commission shall request each such contract from every chief State election official and every known party to one. The Commission shall publish the cap for each kind, stated as a government purchase price, with each contract and price from which it was computed, not later than 90 days after the date of enactment, and where no counted contract states a price for a kind, shall so publish. Where the State or political subdivision became obligated to buy or lease a unit before the cap for its kind was published, or no counted contract states a price for its kind, the cost of the unit is payable without regard to this subsection, and a payment so made is not recovered by reason of a later publication. A published cap that differs from the cap this subsection states does not change it, and in any action concerning a payment or an amount owed under this section the court determines the cap. **The cap applies before the reduction subsection (c) provides. The part of a cost that exceeds the cap is borne by the State or political subdivision that incurred it, and no payment under this section pays it.**\n\n**(d) Each payee claims its own cost.** A political subdivision claims the cost it bore, with notice to the chief State election official, whether or not its State claims, and a State claims the cost it bore. To the extent a cost is paid with funds a State provided to its political subdivision, or a political subdivision provided to its State, for that cost, the government that provided the funds bore it. No cost is paid more than once.\n\n**(e) No requirement on a State.** Except as subsection (e-1) provides for equipment paid for under this section, this section requires no State or political subdivision to examine ballots as subsection (a) describes or to claim a payment, **and does not amend section 301(a)(1)(B) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(1)(B))**. A State or political subdivision that does not claim a payment is subject to no consequence under this section.\n\n**(e-1) The check stays on.** For the period stated in the SCHEDULE after equipment whose acquisition, or whose programming to examine a category under subsection (b), was paid for under this section is placed, the equipment may not be used in an election for Federal office without the examination subsection (a) describes, and ballots cast in person at the place the claim for the payment states under subsection (c-1), or at a place treated under subsection (b) as that place, **of each category under subsection (b) for which the payment was made, and of each category that equipment at the place, or at any place it is treated as under that subsection, so examined on the date stated in the SCHEDULE**, shall be examined as subsection (a) describes. **Neither condition fails as to a ballot not examined because the equipment examining it was inoperable, by a failure that neither the recipient nor any person conducting the election for it chose, where the examination is restored at the place at the next election for Federal office held there.** For purposes of this section, equipment is placed, as to a payment for acquiring it or for programming it to examine a category under subsection (b), when first used, after that acquisition or programming, in an election for Federal office, or at the first such election held at the place the claim states after the payment, whichever is earlier. **Where either condition fails, the recipient shall repay the payment for that equipment in proportion to the part of the period then unexpired.** A recipient shall certify that both conditions were met at each election for Federal office during the period, in each later claim under this section and in a statement delivered to the Commission not later than January 31 following each calendar year in which the period ran, and the statement shall identify each place and election at which ballots were not so examined, the cause, and the election at which the examination was restored; the Commission shall publish each statement on receipt; and an amount owed under this subsection is recovered as subsection (c-1) provides for an amount found not payable.\n\n**(f) Appropriation.** There are appropriated to the Election Assistance Commission, **out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated and without fiscal year limitation**, such sums as are necessary for payments under subsection (a), for interest under this subsection, and for reimbursement under this subsection. **Amounts appropriated by this subsection are available on the date of enactment and remain available until expended. The appropriation is not subject to apportionment delay, reserve, or programmatic withholding and is available notwithstanding sections 1512 and 1513 of title 31, United States Code; an obligation or expenditure of it in the amounts this section requires, including the amount owed on a claim, does not violate section 1517(a) of that title by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less; and a failure to request, apportion, allot, obligate, or disburse the amounts appropriated does not render them unavailable.** Section 1012 of the Impoundment Control Act of 1974 (2 U.S.C. 683) does not apply to the amounts appropriated by this subsection, and section 1013(b) of that Act (2 U.S.C. 684(b)) permits no deferral of them; for purposes of sections 1015 and 1016 of that Act (2 U.S.C. 686, 687), those amounts are budget authority required under that Act to be made available for obligation. In this subsection, the amount owed on a claim is the cost the claim states, reduced as subsections (c) and (c-2) provide unless the claim states it so reduced, and an amount recovered by offset against a payment on the claim, under subsection (c-1) or (e-1), is paid on it to the extent the amount recovered was not payable under this section or was owed under subsection (e-1). Where a State or political subdivision has submitted a claim and the amount owed on it has not been paid in full within 90 days after its submission, **the amount unpaid bears interest at the rate determined under section 1961(a) of title 28, United States Code, for the calendar week preceding the 91st day, computed daily and compounded annually, from the 91st day until it is paid, whether or not a court so orders**, and the State or political subdivision may recover the amount owed on the claim and the interest in an action in the United States Court of Federal Claims. A judgment in such an action is paid under section 1304 of title 31, United States Code, as a judgment for which payment is not otherwise provided, with interest under this subsection to the date of payment notwithstanding section 1304(b) of that title, and the Commission shall reimburse the amount paid from the amounts appropriated by this subsection. A limitation in a later Act on the use of the amounts appropriated by this subsection does not reduce the amount owed on a claim or the interest on it unless that Act refers to this subsection expressly. \u00a7 512 applies to the amounts appropriated by this subsection, and a political subdivision denied a payment under this section in violation of \u00a7 512(a) may bring the action \u00a7 512(b) provides as a State may, and, as so applied, a reference in \u00a7 512(a) to a State includes the political subdivision.\n\n**(g) Authority.** This section is enacted under the power to provide for the general welfare, and subsection (e-1) as a condition on the funds a State or political subdivision elects to receive; as to elections for Federal office, this section is also enacted under the authority stated in \u00a7 904(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-601","heading":"\u00a7 601. What may be obtained, and for what","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 STATE VOTER REGISTRATION RECORDS","body":"**(a) No compelled transfer.** No federal officer may require a State to furnish a voter registration list, or any part of one containing information identifying an individual voter, except pursuant to an order of a court of the United States issued in an action already commenced alleging a violation of federal law by that State, **on a particularized finding that the information is necessary to the action, after notice to the State's chief election official.** A subpoena, a request for production, or any other demand made without such an order does not satisfy this subsection. An order under this subsection is **stayed automatically pending any review the State seeks**, and the information remains in the State's custody during the stay.\n\n**(b) The inspection right is not a transfer right.** The requirement that records concerning list maintenance be available for inspection **confers no right to obtain a copy of the registration list.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 STATE VOTER REGISTRATION RECORDS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-602","heading":"\u00a7 602. Use","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 STATE VOTER REGISTRATION RECORDS","body":"**(a) Purpose limitation.** Voter registration information held by a federal officer or agency, **however obtained** \u2014 from a State, by purchase from any person, under the public-records law of a State, or otherwise \u2014 may be used only for the purpose stated when it was obtained, and **may not be transferred to any other agency, matched against any other federal database, or used for any purpose relating to immigration enforcement, criminal investigation of an individual voter, or the compilation of a list of persons.**\n\n**(b) Destruction.** Information within subsection (a) shall be destroyed on the conclusion of the proceeding or other purpose for which it was obtained, and the destruction certified and published.\n\n**(c) No contrary agreement.** A term of any agreement with a State purporting to permit a use barred by subsection (a) is void."},{"anchor":"sec-602a","heading":"\u00a7 602A. The matching statute applies","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 STATE VOTER REGISTRATION RECORDS","body":"**(a) The regime is extended, because it does not otherwise reach.** A comparison of voter registration information within \u00a7 602(a), however obtained, against records of any Federal agency **shall be treated as a matching program** for purposes of subsections (o), (p), (q) and (u) of section 552a of title 5, United States Code, **and no such comparison may be conducted except in compliance with them.**\n\n**(b) No exemption.** No provision of this Act, of the National Voter Registration Act, or of any appropriation shall be construed to exempt a comparison described in subsection (a) from those provisions.\n\n**(c) Cumulative.** The requirements of this Title are in addition to those of section 552a of title 5, United States Code, and do not displace them."},{"anchor":"sec-603","heading":"\u00a7 603. Enforcement","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 STATE VOTER REGISTRATION RECORDS","body":"A State, a State election official, or a person whose information was transferred may bring an action for injunctive relief, for destruction of the information, and for fees. **The duties in this Title are ministerial.**"},{"anchor":"sec-611","heading":"\u00a7 611. Election officials as at-risk individuals","part":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":"**(a) The extension.** For purposes of **sections 5934 and 5937** of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (28 U.S.C. \u00a7 601 note), the term **\"at-risk individual\"** includes an **election official** and an **election worker**.\n\n**(a-1) These sections and not the others.** The extension in subsection (a) reaches **section 5934 of that Act** (government-agency non-publication and removal, the State and local grant program, and the prohibitions on data brokers and other persons) and **section 5937 of that Act** (the rules of construction, including the protections for reporting and for speech on matters of public concern). The action under section 5934(f) of that Act is confined by its terms to a Federal judge, and the remedy for a person within subsection (a) is the action subsection (e) of this section provides. **It does not reach section 5935 or section 5936 of that Act**, which establish training and functions of the Federal judiciary and have no application to an election official. **The omission of those sections is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(a-2) The exceptions travel with the prohibition.** For purposes of section 5934(a)(3) of that Act, **a reference to a Federal judge includes a person within subsection (a).**\n\n**(a-3) What a notice must contain.** For purposes of section 5934(b)(1) of that Act as applied to a person within subsection (a), **the reference to redress and penalties provided in subsection (f) of that section is a reference to the remedy subsection (e) of this section provides**, and a notice stating that remedy satisfies the requirement. No notice or request is insufficient, and no duty under section 5934(a)(2) or section 5934(d)(2) of that Act fails to attach, by reason of the omission from it of a reference to a remedy that does not apply to that person.\n\n**(a-4) No exception for a sale to a government.** Section 5937(a)(4) and section 5934(d)(1)(B)(ii)(III) of that Act **do not apply to the covered information of a person within subsection (a)** or of an immediate family member of such a person.\n\n**(a-5) The report.** For purposes of section 5934(c)(3)(A)(iii) of that Act, the reference to judges includes election officials and election workers.\n\n**(b) Election official.** An **\"election official\"** is a person who administers, or holds authority over the administration of, an election for Federal office \u2014 including a chief election official of a State, a county, municipal or township election official, a member of a board of elections, a canvassing board or a board of county commissioners acting in an election capacity, and a person who certifies returns.\n\n**(c) Election worker.** An **\"election worker\"** is a person who performs a function in the conduct of an election for Federal office \u2014 including a poll worker, a precinct officer, a ballot processor, a tabulator, an observer designated by an election official, and a person who transports or has custody of ballots \u2014 **whether paid or serving as a volunteer.**\n\n**(d) Former service, and the household.** A person who formerly held either position remains within subsection (a) as to information obtained or a threat arising from that service. The immediate family and household of a person within subsection (a) are reached on the terms section 5933 of that Act provides.\n\n**(e) The remedy.** A person within subsection (a), or an immediate family member of such a person, whose covered information is made public, posted, displayed or transferred as a result of a violation of section 5934 of that Act as extended by this section, or who is threatened with such a violation, **may bring a civil action for declaratory and injunctive relief.** No action, consent or participation of the Attorney General, of the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts, of any chief judge, or of any other officer is a condition of the action. **If a person, business, or association knowingly violates an order granting relief under this subsection, the court issuing the order may impose the fine and award the damages, court costs and reasonable attorney's fees that section 5934(f)(3) of that Act provides**, as though the order had been granted under section 5934(f)(1) of that Act. The action may be brought in a district court of the United States, or, against a defendant other than the United States or an officer or agency of the United States, in any State court of competent jurisdiction. An action against the United States, or against an officer or agency of the United States, may be brought only in a district court of the United States, and **the immunity of the United States from such an action is expressly waived as to the relief this subsection provides**, including the fine and the costs and fees section 5934(f)(3)(A) of that Act provides, **and as to no other relief.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-612","heading":"\u00a7 612. The measures a person may obtain without asking anyone's permission","part":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":"**(a) Published, free, and without application.** The Attorney General shall publish, without charge and **without any application, eligibility determination, or approval** \u2014 a **self-administered residential security survey instrument**, and **personal security training materials** for election officials, election workers, and members of their households \u2014 and shall maintain a means by which any such person may obtain them and may request a review of a completed instrument by a person qualified to conduct a security survey.\n\n**(c) Not a finding.** Obtaining a measure under this section is not a report, not a finding, and not evidence that any risk exists, and **no consequence attaches to obtaining one or to declining to.** No record identifying a person who obtains one may be created, retained, or disclosed, except a record of a requested review under subsection (a) retained only until the review is furnished.\n\n**(d) Ministerial.** The duties in this section are **ministerial and are owed to every election official and election worker**, and are enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus in a district court of the United States by any of them, without regard to whether the duty is owed to the plaintiff within the meaning of section 1361 of title 28, United States Code. The court shall award a prevailing plaintiff reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees and costs."},{"anchor":"sec-613","heading":"\u00a7 613. Money to the States, on a formula, and not at anyone's discretion","part":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":"**(a) The grant, widened.** The grant program under section 5934(c) of that Act, as extended by \u00a7 611, may be used to provide to election officials and election workers the measures described in \u00a7 612(a), residential security equipment and its installation and monitoring, and the redaction or removal of covered information from the records of the State or of its political subdivisions.\n\n**(b) Formula, not discretion.** Amounts appropriated for grants described in subsection (a) shall be **allotted among the States by formula, published in advance**, and **a State meeting the published conditions is entitled to its allotment.** No allotment may be **withheld, conditioned, reduced, delayed, or terminated** except for a failure to meet a condition stated in the appropriation or in this Act, on a written finding published before the action takes effect, **and no such action may be taken by reason of a State's election administration, litigation position, or officials.**\n\n**(c) Consequence.** A State denied an allotment in violation of subsection (b) may bring a civil action for the amounts withheld and for fees, and **\u00a7 902 applies to any authority substituted for one held unavailable.**\n\n**(d) No condition on the State's own law.** Nothing in this Title conditions an allotment on a State adopting any election procedure, and **no measure under this Title may be offered or withheld to obtain a change in a State's election law or administration.**"},{"anchor":"sec-614","heading":"\u00a7 614. A report of concerning behavior becomes a case, and the case gets a disposition","part":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":"**(a) The channel.** The Attorney General shall maintain and publish a channel by which an election official, an election worker, a member of the household of either, a State or local election authority, or a State or local law enforcement agency may report **concerning behavior** \u2014 communication or conduct indicating an unusual or inappropriate interest in such a person or in the conduct of an election, including a threat, an approach, surveillance, a fixation, a grievance pursued beyond the means the law provides, and conduct indicating preparation for an attack. **The channel shall accept a report of conduct the reporting person cannot characterize**, and shall not require that person to state that the conduct is a threat or to assess it.\n\n**(b) The duty.** Upon a report under subsection (a), the Attorney General **shall open a case, assess it, and reach a disposition within the period stated in the SCHEDULE** \u2014 closure, monitoring for a stated period, contact with the person, referral to a mental-health or social-service authority, referral for prosecution, or referral to a court. **The disposition, its date, and the facts on which it rests are recorded, and a case is not disposed of by inaction.** A record stating only the category of disposition does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(c) A case, and no apparatus.** This section authorizes the opening of a case upon a report or upon conduct that has come to the Attorney General's attention, and **authorizes no program of monitoring, scanning, collection, or analysis directed at persons who are not the subject of a case.** **\"Monitoring\"** means the periodic review of information already lawfully held and of any further report, and **does not authorize physical surveillance, the interception of a communication, the acquisition of a record available only on process, the acquisition of a record by purchase, or any covert technique.**\n\n**(d) What may not open a case.** No case may be opened on the basis of a person's speech on a matter of public concern, association, political affiliation, religion, national origin, or petition to a government, except upon concerning behavior as subsection (a) describes it; and **that a person's grievance concerns an election, an election official, or an outcome is not itself concerning behavior.**\n\n**(e) Closed use-list, deletion, and no consequence.** Information in a case may be used only to assess and dispose of that case, to protect a person under this Title, and to make a referral under subsection (b); may not be disseminated for any other purpose or matched against any other database except as a disposition requires; and is deleted, with the deletion certified, after the period stated in the SCHEDULE. That a person is or was the subject of a case **is not a finding of wrongdoing** and may not be used to deny or condition any right, benefit, license, employment, or security clearance. **Information in a case may not be used against the person in any proceeding, or as the basis for any adverse action, unless it is disclosed to that person and the person is afforded an opportunity to respond.**\n\n**(f) Published.** The Attorney General shall publish annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person: cases opened and the source of the report; dispositions by category; time to disposition, including cases exceeding the period under subsection (b); and **the number of cases in which a person who had been the subject of a case thereafter engaged in violence against a person this Title protects.**\n\n**(g) It does not wait on anyone.** The duties in this section are **ministerial**, and are enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by the person on whose behalf the report was made. Nothing in this section conditions a duty on a determination by any officer that a threat is credible, serious, or prosecutable."},{"anchor":"sec-615","heading":"\u00a7 615. What this Title does not do","part":"TITLE VI-A \u2014 PROTECTION OF ELECTION OFFICIALS","body":"**(a) No duty on a State.** Nothing in this Title requires a State or a political subdivision to conduct a survey, provide training, furnish protection, remove information from its own records, or take any other action. **A State's participation in \u00a7 613 is voluntary**, and a State that does not participate is subject to no consequence under this Title.\n\n**(b) No protective detail.** This Title establishes no Federal protective detail for an election official or election worker and confers no authority to provide one. **That omission is a determination of Congress**.\n\n**(c) Authority.** As to elections for Federal office this Title is enacted under the authority stated in \u00a7 904(a); the grant under \u00a7 613 is enacted under the power to provide for the general welfare; and the extension in \u00a7 611, which binds Federal agencies and private persons, is enacted under those authorities and under the power to regulate commerce.\n\n**(d) Severability.** Each section of this Title is severable from each other section. **If \u00a7 611 is held invalid, \u00a7\u00a7 612 through 614 remain in force**; if \u00a7 613 is held invalid, the remainder remains in force and the measures under \u00a7 612 continue to be published. **The extension made by \u00a7 611 is severable as to each section and subsection of that Act to which it applies, and as to each class of person within \u00a7 611(b), (c) and (d)**; the invalidity of the extension as applied to one prohibition, or to one class, does not affect the others."},{"anchor":"sec-701","heading":"\u00a7 701. Certification is ministerial","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 CERTIFICATION","body":"**(a)** The certification of returns for an election for federal office is a **ministerial** duty. An official charged with it **shall certify the returns as reported by the officials responsible for tabulating them.**\n\n**(b) No withheld certification.** An official may not decline, delay, or condition certification on the basis of a belief about the conduct of the election, an allegation of irregularity, a pending investigation, a pending contest, or any matter the law commits to another proceeding.\n\n**(c) The remedy for a contested election is the contest.** A challenge to the conduct of an election proceeds under the procedure the law provides, **and certification is not that procedure.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 CERTIFICATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-702","heading":"\u00a7 702. Consequence","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 CERTIFICATION","body":"**(a) Mandamus by any person.** The duty in \u00a7 701 is enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, and the court **shall** award fees to a prevailing plaintiff.\n\n**(b) Deemed certified.** Where an official has not certified within the period fixed by State law, or, where State law fixes no period, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, **the returns as reported by the tabulating officials are the certified returns by operation of this subsection**, and no further act by any person is required.\n\n**(b-1) The period in force when voting begins.** A change to the period fixed by State law, made after the first day on which any ballot may be cast in an election, **does not apply at that election, whether it lengthens or shortens the period**, and the period in force on that day applies.\n\n**(c) No inference.** Nothing in this Title creates an offense or limits any State law providing a consequence for a failure to certify."},{"anchor":"sec-703","heading":"\u00a7 703. Relation to the certification of presidential electors","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 CERTIFICATION","body":"**(a) That ground is already occupied.** Federal law requires the executive of each State to issue a certificate of ascertainment of appointment of electors by a stated date, and provides an expedited action, heard by a court of three judges and reviewable directly by the Supreme Court, to compel it. **This Title neither displaces nor amends that provision.**\n\n**(b) What it does not reach.** That provision governs **the appointment of presidential electors and no other certification.** It does not reach the certification or canvass of returns for any other Federal office, and it does not reach a county, municipal or other local board whose certification is a step toward a State's own canvass.\n\n**(c) Cumulative.** The duties and remedies of this Title are in addition to that provision, and **a person entitled to relief under it is not required to seek relief under this Title first, or at all.**"},{"anchor":"sec-801","heading":"\u00a7 801. Polling places and drop locations on Tribal lands","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":"**(a)** On the request of an Indian Tribe, a State shall provide not fewer than one polling place and one ballot drop location on the Tribe's Tribal lands, at a site the Tribe designates.\n\n**(b) No reduction without consent.** A State may not reduce, consolidate, relocate or eliminate a polling place or drop location on Tribal lands **without the written consent of the Tribe.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-802","heading":"\u00a7 802. Identification","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":"A State that requires identification to register or to vote **shall accept identification issued by an Indian Tribe**, whether or not it bears an address or an expiration date."},{"anchor":"sec-803","heading":"\u00a7 803. Addresses","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":"**(a)** A State may not deny registration, or reject a ballot, on the ground that a voter's residence lacks a residential street address.\n\n**(b)** A voter residing on Tribal lands may designate any location the voter can describe, including a Tribal building or a described physical location, and may designate a separate mailing address."},{"anchor":"sec-804","heading":"\u00a7 804. Ballot collection","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":"**(a)** A State may not limit the number of ballots a person may collect and return on behalf of voters residing on Tribal lands, and may not require that the person be a family member or household member.\n\n**(b) Designated agents.** A Tribe may designate persons to collect and return ballots, and a State shall accept ballots so returned.\n\n**(c) What a State may require, and what it may not.** A State may require a person returning a ballot for another to sign a record stating the person's name and the number of ballots returned. **A State may not use that record, or any requirement of identification, to limit the number of ballots a person may return, to impose a fee, or to condition acceptance of a ballot on any further showing**, and **a defect in the record is not a ground to reject a ballot.**"},{"anchor":"sec-805","heading":"\u00a7 805. Consultation","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 VOTING ON TRIBAL LANDS","body":"**(a)** A State shall consult each Indian Tribe with Tribal lands in the State before making a change described in \u00a7 104(b) that applies on those lands, and **shall publish the Tribe's response with the change.**\n\n**(b) Consequence.** A change made without the consultation subsection (a) requires **does not apply on those Tribal lands at the election at which it would first apply**, and the practice in force before the change applies there, unless the Tribe consents to the change in writing."},{"anchor":"sec-901","heading":"\u00a7 901. Defenses and construction","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions and limitations available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. **No defense is to be inferred from the subject matter, from the identity of the defendant, or from the absence of an express statement.**\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) Purpose.** This Act is to be construed to give the fullest effect to the participation of eligible voters in elections for federal office.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-902","heading":"\u00a7 902. Anti-substitution","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Where an authority relied upon to exclude a voter, to reject a ballot, to obtain a State's voter registration records, or to place a federal officer at a place described in \u00a7 501, is held unavailable or is determined not to authorize the action taken, **the conditions of this Act apply to any other authority invoked to reach substantially the same result.**\n\n**(b)** An officer invoking a substituted authority after such a holding shall publish a statement identifying the authority previously relied upon, the holding, the authority now invoked, and the difference between the conditions each imposes."},{"anchor":"sec-903","heading":"\u00a7 903. Severability","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Each Title is severable from each other Title and each section from each other section. **Congress would have enacted each Title of this Act independently of the others.**\n\n**In particular: Titles III through VII do not depend on the validity of Title I or Title II, and were Titles I and II held invalid in whole, Congress would have enacted the remainder.** **Title III is drawn to require no racial classification and to operate on an authority independent of the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments.**"},{"anchor":"sec-904","heading":"\u00a7 904. Application, and the authority for each application","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Elections for Federal office.** Every requirement of this Act applies to an election for Federal office. **As to those elections this Act is enacted under the authority of Congress to make or alter regulations respecting the times, places and manner of holding elections for Senators and Representatives**, and, as to the election of electors, under the authority of Congress over the time of choosing them.\n\n**(b) Other elections.** A requirement of this Act applies to an election for State or local office **to the extent stated in the provision imposing it.** As to those elections the provision is enacted under the authority of Congress to enforce the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments.\n\n**(c) Severability by election.** **The application of any provision of this Act to elections for Federal office is severable from its application to any other election.** Were the application of a provision to elections for State or local office held invalid, **the provision remains in force as to elections for Federal office**, and Congress would have enacted it for those elections alone.\n\n**(d) No narrowing by implication.** Subsection (b) does not narrow a provision that by its terms applies to every election, **and the statement of one authority for a provision is not a determination that no other authority supports it.**\n\n**(e) References.** A reference in this Act to a section preceded by the symbol \"\u00a7\" is to a section of this Act. A reference to a numbered section that is not preceded by that symbol and does not identify another Act, a title or code, or a provision of the Constitution is to that section of the Voting Rights Act of 1965."},{"anchor":"sec-905","heading":"\u00a7 905. Actions by aggrieved persons","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) The right and the action.** A person aggrieved by a violation of section 2 as restated by Title I, or of any requirement of Title II, **may bring a civil action** in a district court of the United States for declaratory and injunctive relief and for such other relief as the court finds appropriate, **and the court shall award a prevailing plaintiff reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees and costs.**\n\n**(a-1) Aggrieved.** A person is **aggrieved** for purposes of subsection (a) where the person is subject to, or will be subject to, the practice, procedure, standard or plan complained of, **or is denied information this Act entitles the person to receive.** An organization is aggrieved where a member is, **and need not identify the member by name in the pleading.** **This subsection identifies the persons on whom this Act confers a right; it does not enlarge or restrict the judicial power.**\n\n**(a-2) Pleading.** A complaint states a claim under this section where it alleges facts which, if proved, would establish the elements of the violation. **No heightened standard of pleading applies**, and a court may not require a plaintiff to plead facts within the exclusive knowledge of the defendant, to anticipate a defense, or to plead the absence of a lawful explanation.\n\n**(a-3) Abrogation.** A State, and an instrumentality or official of a State acting in an official capacity, **is subject to suit under this section in Federal or State court for the relief provided**, and **the immunity of the States from such suit is abrogated.** **Congress states that abrogation expressly, and enacts this subsection under section 2 of the Fifteenth Amendment and section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment.**\n\n**(a-4) Severability by defendant.** The application of this section to a State or a State instrumentality or official is severable from its application to every other defendant. **Were the application to a State held invalid, this section remains in force as to every other defendant, and Congress would have enacted it for those defendants alone.**\n\n**(b) Expressly conferred.** The right and the action stated in subsection (a) are **conferred expressly by this section**. **No inference, implication or construction is required to establish either**, and **no presumption against the creation of a private right or a private remedy applies to this section.**\n\n**(c) Also enforceable as a right.** The requirements of Titles I and II are rights within the meaning of section 1979 of the Revised Statutes (42 U.S.C. 1983), and are enforceable in an action under that section. **This subsection is cumulative of subsection (a) and neither is exclusive of the other.**\n\n**(d) No preconditions.** Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy, nor notice to any officer, nor action or inaction by the Attorney General, is a condition of an action under this section. **A determination by the Attorney General not to act is not a bar, an adjudication, or evidence in any action under this section.**\n\n**(e) These and only these.** The limitations on an action under this section are those and only those expressly stated in this Act. **The omission of a limitation is a determination by Congress and not a gap**, and no limitation may be supplied from the enforcement provisions of any other statute, from the assumption of any court that a cause of action arises by implication, or from the absence of an express statement in any earlier enactment.\n\n**(f) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this section may be brought in a district court of the United States **or in any State court of competent jurisdiction.** **(2)** An action under this section brought in a State court **may not be removed to a district court of the United States.** **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear an action under this section on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States. **(5) Actions against a State in its own courts.** The abrogation stated in subsection (a-3) applies to an action under this section brought in a State court. **It rests on section 2 of the Fifteenth Amendment and section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment**, which reach a State's immunity from suit in its own courts, and it does not rest on any power conferred by Article I. **(6) Federal defendants.** An action under this section against the United States, or against an officer or agency of the United States, may be brought only in a district court of the United States, and paragraphs (1) through (3) do not apply to it."},{"anchor":"sec-906","heading":"\u00a7 906. Fee awards","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"An award of attorney's fees or expert fees under any provision of this Act is **payable directly to the attorney or expert**, and is not subject to offset for any debt of the plaintiff."},{"anchor":"sec-907","heading":"\u00a7 907. Fee awards and the deduction for civil-rights claims","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following new paragraph, shown as inserted:\n\n> **(19) Section 905 of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act, and any other provision of that Act under which an award of attorney's fees is made.**"},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"*Every period touching the conduct of an election is measured from the first day on which any ballot may be cast, not from election day, except a value that states the date of the election or a period the provision measures from another event.*\n\n| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 103(a) interim-relief proximity period; stated in \u00a7 103(a) | 90 days before the first day any ballot may be cast | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 104(a) manner and period of publication of voting changes | on a public website maintained by the State's chief election official, in each language in which the jurisdiction provides election materials under section 203 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 and, for an oral or unwritten language, orally; not later than 90 days before the first day any ballot may be cast in the election at which the change first applies | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 202(a) demographic threshold for practice-based coverage | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 302(b) period for the legislature to enact a conforming plan | 30 days after the order holding the plan invalid | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 402(c) receipt period where a postmark is absent or illegible | 7 days after the election | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 403(a) cure period | [___ days after notice] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 404(a) minimum number of secure ballot drop locations | 1 per 15,000 registered voters in each county or equivalent, and not fewer than 1 per county or equivalent and 1 in each city, town and census-designated place with a post office | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 501(a) period of prohibited federal presence; stated in \u00a7 501(a) | from 90 days before the first day any ballot may be cast until certification is complete | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 511(b) window within which a federal directive altering election procedure has no effect | 90 days before the first day any ballot may be cast, through certification | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 514(a) period to publish the data behind a federal fraud assertion | 20 days (excepting Saturdays, Sundays, and legal public holidays) after the assertion | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 203(d) share of voting changes whose sixty-day period ended in a calendar year and elapsed without a determination that constitutes a shortfall; stated in \u00a7 203(d) | 5 percent of the voting changes whose period ended in the calendar year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 614(b) period within which a case concerning an election official shall reach a disposition | 10 days (excepting Saturdays, Sundays, and legal public holidays) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 614(e) retention period for case information following disposition | 5 years; for a case closed without further action, 1 day | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 401(c) latest time by which a State shall accept an application to vote by mail | 7 days before the date of the election | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 702(b) period for certification where State law fixes none | 36 days after the date of the election; for a certification on which another certification depends whose period State law fixes, the day before the last day of that period | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 515(b), (c-1), (c-2), (e-1) date on which the equipment at a place, and the contracts setting the price cap, are measured | [___ the date the bill enacted as this Act was introduced] (COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 515(b), (e-1) period after placement during which maintaining, programming and leasing equipment whose acquisition was payable under \u00a7 515(b) is payable, and equipment whose acquisition or programming was paid for under \u00a7 515 keeps the overvote check on | 10 years | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
